# Why trade if you're better at everything?
subtitle: "How Ricardo's wine and cloth show that trade pays even a country that is better at making both goods than its partner."

## Why trade if you're better at everything?
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Country
    col_label: Hours of labour per unit
    row_values:
      - Portugal
      - England
    col_values:
      - Wine
      - Cloth
    cells:
      - ['80', '90']
      - ['120', '100']
adjust:
    row_header_0:
      scale: 1.5
      move: [-1.5, 0]
    row_header_1:
      scale: 1.5
      move: [-1.5, 0]
    col_header_0:
      scale: 1.5
    col_header_1:
      scale: 1.5
    col_title:
      scale: 1.4
      move: [0, 3]

    portrait portrait_1 of "David Ricardo" cameo true hidden true
    text oc_pt "0.89 cloth" #8a5fa8 x 855 y 435 font_size 32 hidden true
    text oc_en "1.2 cloth" #8a5fa8 x 855 y 285 font_size 32 hidden true
    math gain tex "\\text{Together: } +\\frac{1}{8}\\ \\text{wine},\\ +\\frac{1}{5}\\ \\text{cloth}" colors {"\\frac{1}{8}":"#2f6b8f","\\frac{1}{5}":"#2f6b8f"} x 500 y 168 hidden true
    text oc_head "1 wine =" #8a5fa8 x 855 y 545 font_size 30 hidden true
    text pt_w "+1⅛" #2f6b8f x 388 y 395 font_size 28 hidden true
    text pt_c "−1" #2f6b8f x 617 y 395 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Why trade if you're better at everything?"

    hide row_title

Why would a country that makes everything more cheaply still buy from abroad? The answer came in 1817, from David Ricardo, a rich stockbroker turned economist.
    draw portrait_1

    erase portrait_1

His example: two countries, two goods, and the hours of work each needs for one unit.
    draw grid col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1

Portugal needs eighty hours for a unit of wine, ninety for a unit of cloth.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_0_1

England needs more for both: a hundred and twenty, and a hundred. Portugal is better at everything.
    draw cell_1_0 cell_1_1

So why would Portugal buy anything from England? Because the real price of wine is the cloth you don't make.
    point at.ref cell_0_0 gesture circle

In Portugal, eighty hours of wine could have made eight ninths of a cloth. Wine is cheap there.
    draw oc_head oc_pt

In England, a hundred and twenty hours of wine could have made one point two cloth. Wine is dear there — so cloth is relatively cheap.
    draw oc_en

Now Portugal moves ninety hours out of cloth: one cloth less, and an eighth more than one wine.
    draw pt_c pt_w

England moves a hundred and twenty hours out of wine: one wine less, and one point two cloth more.
    text en_w "−1" #2f6b8f x 388 y 245 font_size 28
    text en_c "+1.2" #2f6b8f x 617 y 245 font_size 28

Same hours, same workers — and together they have more of both goods. Trade then shares the extra out.
    draw gain

What counts is not who is faster, but who gives up less. Even a country worse at everything is relatively best at something.
    highlight oc_pt oc_en

Asked for one idea in social science that is both true and not obvious, Paul Samuelson named this one.
    point at.ref gain gesture underline

    quiz "When would trade between the two countries gain nothing?"
        right: When both give up the same cloth per wine: with equal opportunity costs, no switch of hours makes more of anything.
        * When one is better at both goods
        + When both give up the same cloth per wine
        * When one is worse at both goods
---
